  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35897</id>
    <title>Inverted Gravity</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>James E. Anderson, Mario Larch &amp; Yoto V. Yotov</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We invert structural gravity to recover trade cost changes delivering targeted outcomes. For attainable welfare targets, one instrument per target yields a unique solution. At unchanged producer prices and to first order, each instrument must change by the welfare target divided by its own expenditure share; the required domestic change is about the size of the target, while the import change is several times larger. Inverting observed trade flows gives a separate trade cost change for each of the two directions in which a pair of countries trades. Applications to NAFTA and world trade illustrate the benefits of our approach.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35897"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35896</id>
    <title>When Cash Flows Turn Negative: Liquidity-Driven Selling by Pension Funds</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Aleksandar Andonov, Kristy A. E. Jansen &amp; Joshua Rauh</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Pension funds are long-horizon investors with predictable liability structures. As such, they are often perceived as investors who can provide liquidity and stabilize markets. However, pension funds increasingly face negative operating cash flows as benefit payments exceed contributions. Using aggregate data on U.S. public pension funds combined with granular holdings obtained through public records requests, we document three findings that challenge the conventional view of pension funds as stabilizing investors. First, pension funds with more negative cash flows do not adjust their target asset allocations and maintain low allocations to safe and liquid assets. Second, because of these limited safe asset buffers, pension funds meet cash flow shocks primarily by selling equities, absorbing $0.67 per dollar of shock through equity sales. At the security level, pension funds sell across equities rather than following a safe and liquid-assets-first approach. Third, pension funds sell equities even when equity returns are negative, indicating that these trades reflect liquidity needs rather than portfolio rebalancing. Together, these findings show that pension funds have become regular sellers in equity markets rather than contrarian, stabilizing investors.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35896"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35895</id>
    <title>Do Judges Move Markets? Ideological Shifts in the Supreme Court and Macroeconomic Expectations</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Thomas Drechsel &amp; Naixin Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper studies how judicial ideology shapes financial markets' expectations about the macroeconomy. We use unexpected deaths of U.S. Supreme Court Justices as quasi-experimental shifts in the Court's ideological balance. High-frequency asset price responses reveal how markets perceive the macroeconomic consequences of these ideological shifts. We find that a more conservative Court is associated with lower inflation expectations, lower real activity expectations, and lower bond yields. A shift towards a more liberal Court induces the opposite responses. The finding that inflation and activity expectations respond with the same sign suggests that markets revise their expectations about aggregate demand, rather than aggregate supply. Moreover, contrasting the yield responses with estimates from studies of fiscal policy news indicates that a more conservative (liberal) Court leads markets to expect tighter (looser) future fiscal policy. We validate our quasi-experimental setup with sectoral asset price dynamics, such as high-frequency responses of clean energy and health care stocks. Taken together, our findings are consistent with markets interpreting changes in the Supreme Court's ideological composition as news about future fiscal expansions or contractions.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35895"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35894</id>
    <title>Estimating the Impact of Targeted Public Policies with Limited Data</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Kelly M. Jones &amp; Mayra Pineda-Torres</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Many public policies aim to support (or change the behavior of) a specific, targeted sub-group of the population. To measure the causal impacts of such policies, social scientists use quasi-experimental methods of policy evaluation, but are often hampered by data availability issues, especially regarding targeted populations, who are often disadvantaged and/or vulnerable. We use power calculations to quantify the challenge of detecting a policy’s impact on a small minority population based on population level data, using the impact of abortion bans on economic welfare as an example. We provide insight into this challenge and a roadmap for how researchers can calculate, ex-ante, whether their study is sufficiently powered to detect reasonably meaningful effect sizes.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35894"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35893</id>
    <title>Affirmative Action in Centralized College Admissions Systems</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Nano Barahona, Cauê Dobbin &amp; Sebastián Otero</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study the consequences of affirmative action in college admissions. We develop an empirical framework that distinguishes the effects on marginally benefited and displaced students from the overall effects of the policy. The overall effects include equilibrium reallocations as benefited students leave seats that others fill and displaced students take seats elsewhere, displacing others in turn. We apply this framework to a large-scale policy in Brazil that required all federal institutions to reserve half their seats for racially and socioeconomically disadvantaged applicants. Exploiting admissions cutoffs, we find that marginally benefited targeted students attend and graduate from higher-quality programs. Their graduation gains are about twice the losses of marginally displaced non-targeted students, reflecting targeted students’ weaker outside options. To evaluate the overall effects, we estimate a model of degree choices and potential outcomes. Among all reallocated students, benefited students still gain nearly twice what displaced students lose, but equilibrium reallocations make the policy displace about 30% more students than it benefits. Non-targeted students’ losses therefore offset about two-thirds of targeted students’ graduation gains, leaving a modest net gain across all students.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35893"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35892</id>
    <title>Strategic Complementarity and Persistence in Linear-Quadratic Mean Field Games</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Fernando E. Alvarez &amp; David Argente</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study the relation between strategic complementarities and aggregate persistence in linear-quadratic mean field games with interactions in states and actions. The framework provides local approximations to common macroeconomic problems, including capital accumulation and multiproduct pricing. We show that the equilibrium aggregate dynamics coincide with those of a single-agent linear-quadratic control problem in which state and action curvatures are adjusted for strategic interactions. This result gives conditions for equilibrium existence, establishes uniqueness, and links strategic interactions to aggregate adjustment. We establish conditions under which stronger state complementarities increase persistence, while stronger action complementarities reduce it, even in multidimensional problems whose underlying matrices are not simultaneously diagonalizable. Under these conditions, internalizing state complementarities makes the utilitarian planner’s allocation more persistent, while internalizing action complementarities makes it less persistent. With common shocks, we give conditions under which individual and aggregate dynamics can be combined to identify strategic interactions and model parameters. Finally, we extend the analysis to interactions through higher moments of the state distribution.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35892"/>
