    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35879</id>
    <title>How Knowledge Crosses Borders: Evidence from Semiconductor Manufacturing</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Pinelopi Koujianou Goldberg, Réka Juhász, Giulia Lo Forte &amp; Jeff Thurk</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We show that cross-border learning is an important feature of modern semiconductor manufacturing and that several important channels – such as knowledge sharing along global supply chains, the movement of engineers within multinational firms, and foreign direct investment – operate through market mechanisms rather than anonymous technological spillovers. These findings have significant policy implications. When cross-border learning is mediated by purposeful economic relationships, access to frontier knowledge is neither automatic nor guaranteed. Firms and governments can facilitate - or restrict - the international diffusion of technology, implying that policies to foster domestic technological upgrading are likely to be most effective when coupled with continued access to global sources of frontier knowledge.</content>
