  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35926</id>
    <title>The Why and How of Foreign Aid</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Dean Karlan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Foreign aid has become a melting pot of motivations and policies, where differently-motivated actors often converge on recommended policies while the similarly-motivated diverge. Breaking down the “why” and the “how” can illuminate real tensions and reveal false ones. The “why” asks which problems to tackle and who benefits: the recipient, the donor through economic, political, or soft-power returns, or everyone through global public goods. The “how” asks which strategy most cost-effectively addresses a chosen problem. Evidence is central to the “how”: use it where it exists, produce it where it does not. In particular, approaches which improve state capacity to use and generate evidence can satisfy multiple objectives: strengthen recipient countries, benefit donors, and provide a global public good of knowledge. Ultimately, the “how” is not a linear process, but rather an iterative one of learning, adopting, adapting, repeating, and then sharing so others can learn as well.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35926"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35925</id>
    <title>Correcting for Heterogeneity in the Use of Subjective Scales</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Daniel J. Benjamin, Kristen B. Cooper, Ori Heffetz, Miles S. Kimball &amp; Jiannan Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Analyses of survey data may be confounded if people use response scales differently. We formalize and generalize the theory behind using “calibration questions,” such as vignettes, to correct for scale-use heterogeneity. Finding, in our (N ≈ 10,000) and existing data, that the mapping between respondents’ scales is approximately linear, we develop a simple estimator for scale-use-corrected regressions. We validate it using subjective-scale ratings of height and weight and illustrate it with life satisfaction regressions, where correction leaves the main qualitative findings intact but can substantially change coefficient ratios, for example tripling the estimated cost of unemployment relative to income.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35925"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
