  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35906</id>
    <title>Home-Foreign Elasticity Functions and the Gains from Trade</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jonathan Vogel</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Gains from trade are typically calculated using a constant trade elasticity estimated from substitution across foreign countries. For general homothetic preferences, we show how to calculate these gains from the initial domestic expenditure share and the home-foreign elasticity function along a price path to autarky. While this function generally depends on the specific price path, under weak separability it does not. We provide conditions for its nonparametric identification and estimate sector-level elasticity functions using tariff changes implied by trade agreements. Across WIOD and OECD ICIO data, estimated home-foreign elasticities generally rise with domestic expenditure shares. Using estimated elasticity functions in a multi-sector model with intermediate inputs, the median gain across the WIOD countries is 19.37 log points, compared with 24.43 when each country-sector elasticity is held fixed at its initial value.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35906"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
