  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35948</id>
    <title>fevc: Leave-Out Bias-Corrected Variance Decompositions in Stata</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Johannes F. Schmieder</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Variance decompositions from regressions with worker and firm fixed effects are widely used to measure heterogeneity and sorting in linked data. Variances computed directly from estimated effects overstate heterogeneity because they include estimation noise, and the bias can be large when few workers move between firms. This article introduces fevc, a Stata command that implements the leave-out bias correction of Kline, Saggio, and Sølvsten (2020, “Leave-Out Estimation of Variance Components,” Econometrica 88: 1859–1898). fevc reports corrected variances of worker and firm effects, their covariance, and the variance of their sum. Researchers choose the unit left out, the treatment of controls, and the weights that define the population being described. The command also reports standard errors for projections of fixed effects on observed characteristics. It has two backends: a source-only Mata implementation that runs wherever Stata runs, and a parallel Rust plugin for large datasets. Calculations based on random projections report Monte Carlo standard errors that measure their numerical precision. In a public extract of Italian social-security records, the correction halves the estimated variance of firm effects and reverses the sign of the worker–firm covariance. In synthetic benchmarks with 6.4 million observations and 28 threads, the Rust backend is 1.3 to 3.5 times as fast as the Matlab package of Kline, Saggio, and Sølvsten. Under match deletion, its measured peak memory is about 30 percent of the Matlab package’s.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35948"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35947</id>
    <title>An LLM Workflow for Functional Form Sensitivity Analysis</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Isaiah Andrews, Simon C. Essig Aberg &amp; Jesse M. Shapiro</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We introduce an LLM workflow for functional form sensitivity analysis. A researcher estimates the value of an object of interest by maximizing the fit of a working model to some data. The working model assumes an arbitrary form for some function. We define sensitivity by how much it is possible to change the estimate of the object of interest, without compromising fit, by selecting a different functional form. The workflow first looks for an analytic solution. If it fails to find one, it approximates the solution via numerical methods that respect the fit constraint at each step of a solver path. The workflow yields a verifiable lower bound on the in-sample sensitivity of the researcher’s estimate, and is fast in many practical situations. We illustrate with an application to the welfare cost of adverse selection in health insurance.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35947"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35946</id>
    <title>The Value of (Sub) Specialization: Evidence from Oncology</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>René Karadakic, David C. Chan Jr, Nancy L. Keating, Bruce E. Landon &amp; Michael L. Barnett</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We estimate the returns to specialization in medical oncology, in which subspecialists focus on specific cancer types. Using 2.2 million Medicare chemotherapy episodes from 2008–2020, we instrument for subspecialist access with differential distance to cancer-type-specific subspecialists versus general oncologists. Access to a relevant subspecialist reduces three-year mortality by 4.5 percentage points (10 percent), without increasing Medicare spending or measured provider fragmentation. Subspecialists also increase the use of newer therapies and diagnosis-specific clinical trials. These findings show that specialization raises productivity when expertise is closely matched to the task.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35946"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35945</id>
    <title>Homework in Climate Economics: Household Production, Environmental Preferences, and Climate Policy</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Stephie Fried, David Lagakos &amp; Hannah Rhodenhiser</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Household energy use accounts for approximately one third of U.S. emissions. Guided by a new survey, we develop a quantitative model in which households differ in income and their desire to reduce their emissions. Our analysis of cost-minimizing subsidies for clean equipment, such as electric vehicles and heat pumps, and residential solar yields three insights. First, solar receives the highest subsidy rate and accounts for over one third of abatement, reflecting its complementarities with clean equipment. Second, restricting subsidy eligibility to lower-income households generates only small cost savings. Third, without environmental preferences, household emissions would be over 15 percent higher.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35945"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35944</id>
    <title>Selective Admissions in Nursing Homes: Evidence from a Randomized Audit Study</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Liran Einav, Amy Finkelstein, Jesse Gubb, Neale Mahoney &amp; James Okun</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;It has been widely documented that patients in the US who are associated with disadvantaged demographics receive care at lower quality nursing homes. To understand the role that selective admissions may play in contributing to this pattern, we conducted a nationwide audit study of the impact of patient race, payer, and medical condition on responses to about 5,500 availability inquiries sent to about 3,000 nursing homes. We find 10-15% lower availability for Medicaid patients compared to private-pay patients and for patients with Alzheimer’s disease compared to those without; these availability gaps generally increase with nursing home quality. We find no statistically significant evidence of differential availability by race. To quantify the implications of these selective admissions practices, we embed the audit study results in a model of constrained nursing home choice. We estimate that if, counterfactually, Medicaid patients had the same choice sets as their private-pay counterparts, they would attend higher quality nursing homes on average, but the private-Medicaid gap in quality would be reduced by only 5-10%. Our results suggest that supply-side availability differences by payer are widespread, but that they are unlikely to be the primary factor behind the observed gap in quality of nursing homes attended.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35944"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35943</id>
    <title>Global Entrepreneurial Talent Flows: Widely Funded, Selectively Successful?</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Paul A. Gompers &amp; Henry Woo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Immigrant founders of US venture-backed startups exit successfully more often than native founders. Does this hold worldwide? Using career histories for 913,226 founder-firm pairs in 213 countries (1990–2026), we compare founders who build abroad (expats) or return home after foreign experience (returnees) with comparable local founders in the same country. Both expats and returnees raise early funding more often globally. Expats' success premium, however, concentrates in the US (18 percent) and UK (26 percent), especially among those entering the US through graduate school, work, or direct founding. Returnees rarely show such a premium. Capital follows mobile founders across borders, but success follows them selectively.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35943"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35942</id>
    <title>Comparative Advantage and Economies of Scale under Uncertainty</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Xiao Ma &amp; Natalia Ramondo</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study how regional specialization patterns and welfare are affected by uncertainty and economies of scale in an open economy. We use a spatial equilibrium model with sectoral economies of scale, aggregate uncertainty, and irreversible mobility decisions made by heterogenous workers. We analytically characterize how the interactions between comparative advantage, both of workers and firms, economies of scale, and uncertainty shape specialization patterns and, hence, the gains from trade. We find empirical support for the model predictions by documenting the impact of aggregate changes in volatility of sectoral productivity on U.S. regional economies. We calibrate the model using detailed data on U.S. commuting zones and international trade, and extending hat-algebra methods to accommodate uncertainty. Quantitatively, we find that uncertainty shifts employment away from riskier sectors and locations, relative to a deterministic benchmark, lowering the U.S. aggregate gains from trade by around 20 percent. The interaction between uncertainty and scale economies brings down the benefits of specialization. For regions that would otherwise lose from trade, the presence of agglomeration forces and a more elastic labor supply mitigates those losses.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35942"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35940</id>
    <title>Sex and Subsistence: Income Effects on Risky Sexual Behavior and Partner Selection</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Natalie Bau, Corinne Low &amp; Bryce Millett Steinberg</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Whether to engage in sex and with whom are extremely consequential decisions for young women worldwide, and particularly in sub-Saharan Africa, where HIV rates are high and adolescent pregnancies are common. Yet little is known about the extent to which these decisions are financially motivated. We combine a randomized controlled trial of monthly cash assistance to female students at the University of Zambia with detailed, high-frequency data on sexual behavior and partner characteristics. We find that this temporary increase in income significantly changes women's sexual behavior. Treated participants are 5.1 percentage points less likely to have sex during the treatment period, and the rate of condomless sex falls by 30%. Among women who were not sexually active at baseline, the likelihood of having sex falls by 43%, and partner quality conditional on sex greatly improves. Financial assistance also reduces financial precarity, increasing food security, mental health, and cognitive capacity. These results can be rationalized with a dynamic model of partner search in which women can choose to search for boyfriends or high-income partners who provide financial support and bargain over both transfers and condom use.  We conclude that a substantial share of sexual activity is financially motivated in this setting, even in a population not typically regarded as vulnerable. Increasing young women's income may be a useful tool for reducing the negative externalities of risky sex and improving match quality.</content>
